00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.920
There is a hugely smart design teaching as to&nbsp;
why this design here is so vastly superior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.200
this design. And we're going to learn why soon in&nbsp;
this video alongside three other hidden thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.720
teachings as well. All with tutorial examples.&nbsp;
But first, we need to look at Apple. And no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.840
I don't mean the fruit. I mean, of course, Apple&nbsp;
Inc. And they are one of the clearest examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.600
of the first hidden way of thinking as a truly&nbsp;
developed designer. So most people assume that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.200
Apple's graphic assets look good because of the&nbsp;
photography, the typography, all the ample amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.600
of white space they are known for. But those are&nbsp;
just the final decisions. The interesting part is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.880
the thinking that comes before them, which&nbsp;
we're going to look at right now. So every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.840
design decision here with Apple starts with a&nbsp;
question. What helps people understand and desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.120
this product? They're not trying to impress&nbsp;
another designer on behans. They're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.400
convince millions and millions of people that this&nbsp;
product is worth buying. And let's be honest here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:12.560
at currently insane prices these days. Either you&nbsp;
buy the iPhone 18 or you buy a decent Honda Civic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.640
But anyway, Apple removes anything that competes&nbsp;
with your attention for this product. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.680
the hidden lesson. You might start by building&nbsp;
a layout and then hope it works for your client.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.000
But Apple starts with the communication problem&nbsp;
first and only adds what's necessary to solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.960
it. But how do you take that onto your designs? I&nbsp;
hope you're not feeling too hungry because right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.160
here we have a design for a burger advertising&nbsp;
poster. Perhaps one found on an underground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.560
subway or a train station. And so rather&nbsp;
than asking how can I make this look better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.840
I'm going to ask what is the communication&nbsp;
problem here just like we did with Apple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.200
So, in this case, it's getting people to notice&nbsp;
the burger and to quickly understand the offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.560
Things are just way too crowded and crazy. So, the&nbsp;
burger becomes larger because it's the hero. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.320
the thing you want. Unnecessary graphics disappear&nbsp;
because they're competing for attention. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.760
typography is simplified so the message can be&nbsp;
understood in a couple of seconds. You should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.520
simply understand that none of these changes are&nbsp;
about decoration or my personal taste or style.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.880
They're all about making the communication&nbsp;
clearer. And ironically, that usually makes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:33.440
design look better in the first place. The second&nbsp;
hidden layer of design thinking is this. Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:40.480
design creates a feeling before it communicates&nbsp;
information. Fact. So, think about it. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.440
standing on a busy underground platform and that&nbsp;
burger poster from earlier catches your eye for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.680
perhaps 2 seconds as the train arrives. In that&nbsp;
time, you're probably not reading the topography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.040
very much or analyzing the offer. Instead, your&nbsp;
brain has already decided whether this feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:03.040
premium, healthy, playful, cheap, or whatever.&nbsp;
And of course, with good branding, you'd also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.320
notice the brand. But we're not talking about that&nbsp;
right now. The emotional judgment happens almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:14.160
instantly, long before you've consciously process&nbsp;
the information. Great designers understand this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.400
which is why they carefully shape the feeling&nbsp;
someone experiences within the first second of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.800
looking at design. So depending on research&nbsp;
for this burger or fast food brand, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.640
need to tailor that feeling in different ways.&nbsp;
For example, a poster for McDonald's is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:35.920
to be vastly different in style to that of Gordon&nbsp;
Ramsay's Burger Restaurant, which I actually went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:43.360
to many, many years ago, and I can confirm it was&nbsp;
topnotch. But we can take the same burger for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:50.560
design here and we can make it appear cheap using&nbsp;
layout, color and typography choices. Or we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.600
conversely make it feel more expensive and more&nbsp;
premium. And this is achieved with the visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.200
language decisions alone. You must consider&nbsp;
how typography psychology affects a person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:07.440
how color schemes and layouts and even things like&nbsp;
solid color or gradients all influence perception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:14.640
And this is at the very core of graphic design or&nbsp;
shall I say good graphic design. But just look how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.920
quick and how easy it is to take the same design&nbsp;
and how to totally change its target audience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:26.720
approach. And we can even add a more clever and&nbsp;
more cool concept to the premium design too. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.640
like that. But to reiterate and to fully achieve&nbsp;
this, you must understand the audience and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.920
brand so you can then apply the correct feeling&nbsp;
that will actually resonate with them. It is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.280
good trying to make your design premium if the&nbsp;
end users are teenagers or students with little to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:51.280
no money at the disposal. But before a design can&nbsp;
create any feeling at all, it has to win something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:57.360
even more valuable, and that is your attention.&nbsp;
Because if nobody notices the most important part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.920
first, it doesn't really matter how correct the&nbsp;
typography or even how carefully you've chosen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.680
your palette. And that brings us to the third&nbsp;
layer behind good or great graphic design. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:13.920
great graphic designers create hierarchy before&nbsp;
they create or consider beauty as such. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:19.360
let's improve this poster again, but this time I'm&nbsp;
not thinking about colors or typography or making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:26.080
it look more premium. I'm just going to ask one&nbsp;
question. What should somebody notice first? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.760
it's the burger and so that becomes larger. Of&nbsp;
course, the offer is the second most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.400
thing here. So, I'm going to increase the size&nbsp;
and the contrast. Everything else is going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.560
pushed to the background because it supports the&nbsp;
message rather than leading it or just confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.160
it. In fact, one of the easiest ways to create&nbsp;
hierarchy isn't making important things look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:52.160
instantly bigger. It's just making unimportant&nbsp;
things quieter or just completely disappeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.520
And once there's a clear path for the eye to&nbsp;
follow, the design suddenly feels more organized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:03.440
more intentional, and rather conveniently&nbsp;
more beautiful as well. And here's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:09.760
professional designers think about very early on,&nbsp;
viewing distance. This poster is going to be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:16.080
from several meters away on an underground&nbsp;
platform, not from 30 cm away in Photoshop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.520
So before I touch the topography, I want to ask&nbsp;
myself what somebody needs to recognize from 10 m,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.640
5 m, and finally 1 m. And that can actually&nbsp;
immediately tell you how big the burger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:33.440
the headline, supporting information, and&nbsp;
so on should be. It's all about context. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.920
let's go back to this burger poster one last time.&nbsp;
Rather than asking whether you like this version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:46.320
question every single element if you want to&nbsp;
make changes. Why is this text this size? Why is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.560
this image positioned the way it is? Why choose&nbsp;
this color for the offer? That simple habit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.840
forcing every decision to earn its place. That is&nbsp;
often the difference between a design that simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.840
looks good and then one that generally does work.&nbsp;
And one exercise I'd really encourage you to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.120
is just this. After you finish a design or you&nbsp;
think it's finished, pretend you're presenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.480
it to a client and explain every decision out&nbsp;
loud. If you struggle to justify something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.040
you've probably just discovered the weakest part&nbsp;
of your design. You can thank me later for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.800
one. And this is how good and great designers&nbsp;
keep improving day after day, year after year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

